# Repo-root transient cache (release-receipt.jsonl, ad-hoc run-chains receipts written to
# cwd/.cache). Anchored to the repo root so the tracked per-project / archive
# kaola-workflow/**/.cache/ evidence is unaffected.
/.cache/
# Oracle Kernel (kaola-workflow-adaptive-schema.js): ONE canonical source in scripts/, with the
# three per-forge tree copies GENERATED from it (`node scripts/edition-sync.js --materialize-kernel`,
# folded into `--write` and the forge test chains) and COMMITTED — the same generated-but-committed
# posture as every other forge aggregator port.
#
# Their parity is enforced by TWO checks, both in `validate-script-sync.js`: the
# 'adaptive-schema kernel copies' byte group (working tree) and `checkCommittedKernelParity()`, which
# compares the four COMMITTED git blobs and is also called from `edition-sync.js --check` so the
# gitlab/gitea chains cover it. The committed check is the load-bearing one: every chain begins with
# `--materialize-kernel`, which rewrites a drifted forge copy from canonical, so a working-tree
# comparison alone is always repaired before it runs and a drifted COMMIT — the bytes a consumer
# clones — goes unseen. This note previously credited `edition-sync.js --check` alone; it did not
# check the kernel at all, and a one-line edit to the gitea copy passed every guard.
#
# They are committed and NOT ignored because the Codex install path is `git clone` + `codex plugin
# marketplace add <clone>`, which runs no post-clone step: the plugin scripts resolve the kernel as a
# `__dirname` sibling, so a gitignored copy made every Codex plugin entrypoint in a fresh clone die
# with "Cannot find module './kaola-workflow-adaptive-schema'". Ignoring them also made a `git pull`
# of a kernel change leave a stale local copy silently paired with new scripts. Do not re-add them
# here; regenerate with `npm run sync:editions` instead.
